<Project>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Directory.Build.targets, and why this component needs one at all
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    MSBuild looks Directory.Build.targets up SEPARATELY from Directory.Build.props and
    stops at the first one it finds walking up. So the moment this file exists, a project
    under src/ or tests/ stops seeing Broiler.Browser's — including the configuration-name
    DECLARATION that file is the only carrier of. Chaining explicitly is what keeps the
    in-tree build answering exactly as it did before this component was extracted. What the
    parent contributes depends on which tree is above: an aggregate checkout adds ItemGroups
    scoped to other components' project paths, which cannot match anything here, and
    Broiler.Browser adds the same configuration mapping this file imports below — guarded by
    $(BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied), so importing it from both sides is inert.

    The cost of getting this wrong is silent: a component whose Directory.Build.targets
    stopped the lookup instead of chaining compiled its whole project graph UNOPTIMISED
    under Release-VM, and nothing reported it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerParentBuildTargets>$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.targets', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))</BroilerParentBuildTargets>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)" Condition="'$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)' != ''" />

  <!--
    Making the names BEHAVE is Directory.Build.props; making them EXIST is here, and the
    two cannot share an import. This one has to be unconditional and has to be last: a
    project that spells out its own <Configurations> in the csproj body overwrites
    anything the props side set, and this is the first point that sees the result. The
    imported file carries the rest of the reasoning, and its append is idempotent, so
    running after a parent's copy adds nothing a second time.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.ConfigurationNames.targets" />

</Project>
